/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Oracle sync service
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

/// Which leg of the VM sync service a run belongs to. The three are recorded
/// separately because they fail independently — schedules can stop flowing
/// while the roster keeps arriving, and a single "last sync" timestamp would
/// hide that.
enum SyncKind {
  /// Oracle employee + barcode data into CloudTime.
  ROSTER
  /// Oracle `dailyworkerschedule` rows into CloudTime.
  SCHEDULE_PULL
  /// The last security-gate scan per badge, from Oracle `timestationscanlog`.
  /// Seeds the IN/OUT baseline that `scan_events` alternates from, so the
  /// first gate scan after go-live is not forced to resolve IN.
  GATE_STATE
}

/// Outcome of one sync run. PARTIAL is the common real-world case: most rows
/// applied, some were refused or conflicted, and the run itself did not fail.
enum SyncRunStatus {
  RUNNING
  SUCCEEDED
  PARTIAL
  FAILED
}

/// Where a card came from. The sync only ever adds rows, so nothing is at risk
/// of being overwritten today; this records provenance so a later cleanup of
/// stale Oracle cards can tell them apart from one a person entered by hand
/// Which legacy call a tablet was making when it reported an outcome.
enum LegacySyncKind {
  /// PUT .../scantime — the timecard write. This is the one that costs pay.
  PUNCH
  /// GET .../scantimesecurity — the gate write.
  GATE_SCAN
  /// A gate scan taken during an outage and replayed later, carrying its
  /// original timestamp.
  CAPTURE
}

/// How a tablet's conversation with Oracle ended.
///
/// Deliberately not a boolean. REFUSED and FAILED_PERMANENT both mean the
/// punch is not in Oracle, but they need opposite responses: the first is a
/// rule disagreeing, the second is a delivery that never succeeded, and
/// treating them alike is how a broken tablet hides behind a policy.
enum LegacySyncOutcome {
  /// Oracle took it.
  SUCCESS
  /// Oracle understood it and said no — a business rule, most concretely
  /// CA2's warehouse-10 lunch rule. Retrying will never change it.
  REFUSED
  /// Failed and will be tried again.
  RETRYING
  /// Out of attempts or too old to chase. Nobody will try again, and if the
  /// punch was accepted by CloudTime, the two systems have now diverged.
  FAILED_PERMANENT
}

/// How strictly a building applies "scan at security before you clock in".
///
/// <p>Three states rather than a boolean, because the middle one is what makes
/// the rule deployable. Measured 2026-09-21 at NJ299, the only site with
/// working gate readers: 48% of morning clock-ins had a gate IN first. Turning
/// enforcement on would have refused roughly 550 people in a week — not because
/// they skipped the gate on purpose, but because two thirds of them got into
/// the building without the reader seeing them and the evening shift is at 7%.
///
/// <p>REPORT closes that gap without anybody being turned away. A site is
/// measured for as long as it takes for the number to climb, and enforcement is
/// switched on once the report goes quiet — which makes it an observation
/// rather than a bet.
enum GateScanMode {
  /// The rule does not apply. No refusal, no measurement.
  OFF
  /// Measured, never refused. Nobody at a tablet notices any difference.
  REPORT
  /// Refused, subject to the gate still being alive.
  ENFORCE
}

/// while holding the physical badge.
enum BadgeSource {
  ORACLE
  MANUAL
}

/// Which system last asserted a schedule row.
enum ScheduleSource {
  ORACLE
  CLOUDTIME
}

/// How a schedule row stands against Oracle.
///
/// There is no "pushed" state because CloudTime cannot write to Oracle at all:
/// the bridge reaches a read-only standby (WMS_STBY) and Oracle refuses writes
/// inside a read-only transaction. A CloudTime-side edit therefore lives here
/// and nowhere else, which is precisely why it has to be marked — otherwise
/// the next pull would silently overwrite it with Oracle's older value.
enum ScheduleSyncState {
  /// Matches Oracle as of the last pull.
  SYNCED
  /// Edited in CloudTime. Oracle still holds its own value and cannot be told.
  /// Protected from being overwritten by a pull that carries the same Oracle
  /// value we already knew about.
  LOCAL_EDIT
  /// Oracle changed this day too, after CloudTime had edited it. Deliberately
  /// NOT resolved — a schedule row gates gate admission, so the system flags
  /// the disagreement and a human picks the winner.
  CONFLICT
}

/// State of an Oracle employee the sync has seen but CloudTime does not have.
enum RosterCandidateStatus {
  /// Seen, not yet triaged.
  NEW
  /// Deliberately not wanted in CloudTime (contractor, other site, duplicate).
  IGNORED
  /// An Employee now exists for this empId.
  RESOLVED
}

/// How a scan's `direction` was arrived at — whether it is a fact or a guess.
///
/// Worth recording because the two are not close in quality. Replaying two
/// months of production logs, direction taken from the source column is right
/// by construction, while direction guessed by alternation was wrong for 19.9%
/// of time-clock events. A consumer that cannot tell them apart has to treat
/// every row as suspect.
enum ScanDirectionSource {
  /// The legacy column stated it: TIMECLOCKIN / SCANTIME are arrivals,
  /// TIMECLOCKOUT / OUTTIME are departures. Never re-resolved.
  SOURCE_COLUMN
  /// Inferred by alternating from this badge's previous scan in this stream.
  /// The only kind `reresolveFollowing` may rewrite.
  ALTERNATION
  /// Planted as a starting baseline rather than observed at a reader.
  SEEDED
  /// Written by the nightly auto-close for someone who never scanned out.
  /// Not an inference that might be wrong — a record that no exit ever arrived,
  /// and the thing the "who is not clocking out" report counts.
  AUTO_CLOSE
  /// The same badge read again at the same kind of reader within seconds, which
  /// is the reader firing twice rather than the person crossing twice. The row
  /// is kept — it is what the gate actually did, and Oracle records both
  /// crossings too, so dropping it would make the two impossible to reconcile —
  /// but it inherits the previous scan's direction instead of alternating off
  /// it. Measured 2026-09-19: of 447 consecutive gate scan pairs since Sep 17,
  /// 164 were under a minute apart and 160 of those flipped the direction,
  /// leaving people showing as inside a building they had just walked out of.
  REREAD
}
